    "q-28634": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28634",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{x}}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}))\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({e}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
