    "q-313291": {
      "dom_id": "step-q313291",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "If $y=2{x}^{4}(5x+3)^{2},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$(5x+2)(5x+3)$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2{x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=(5x+3)^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=8{x}^{3},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=10(5x+3).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3})(5x+3)^{2}+(2{x}^{4})(10(5x+3)) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}(5x+3)^{2}+20{x}^{4}(5x+3) \\\\  & =[8(5x+3)+20x]{x}^{3}(5x+3) \\\\  & =[60x+24]{x}^{3}(5x+3) \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}(5x+2)(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
