    "q-11938": {
      "dom_id": "step-q11938",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))+(x)(\\frac{1}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
